  "description": "The Pobal HP Deprivation Index for 2006, by Trutz Haase and Jonathan Pratschke for Pobal, calculated from Census 2006, for the Electoral Divisions of the Republic of Ireland. Each ED is shaded by its relative index category. The relative score is scaled so that 0 is the national average: a lower (more negative) score is more disadvantaged and a higher score more affluent. The categories are Pobal's 10-point bands, from 1 'extremely disadvantaged' (below -30, darkest) through 'marginally below average' (-10 to 0) and 'marginally above average' (0 to 10) to 8 'extremely affluent' (30 and above, lightest). Clicking an ED shows the score, Pobal's label, the population and the census indicators behind the index (age dependency, lone parents, primary-only and third-level education, and male and female unemployment). The relative score compares each ED with the rest of the State in 2006 only, so it says which areas are more or less deprived than others, not how deprived an area is in absolute terms. It cannot be compared across years, and it cannot be compared with the Northern Ireland Multiple Deprivation Measure, which ranks areas on different indicators (there, rank 1 is the most deprived). Pobal publishes Tacumshin and Tomhaggard (published as Tacumshin) as one unit, so each of those EDs shows the combined figure (field Pobal_merged). The figures are drawn on Civgraph's 2006 Electoral Division map (eds-2006, the CSO census EDs; 3,409 EDs), matched to Pobal's own ED map by area. Every one of the 3,409 mapped units has a value; 3,403 overlap their Pobal unit by 90% or more, and the rest, a few city EDs, by at least 80%, the difference being the two maps' generalisation. Source: Pobal HP Deprivation Index, Electoral Division 2006 (Pobal, https://www.arcgis.com/home/item.html?id=c4676b5f35324031a39d6ffca08ef0b7), licensed under Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 (CC BY 4.0).",
